## 8. Intended Contribution

OpenTherm Health is not intended to develop a clinically validated medical device.

Instead, the project aims to provide an open and transparent engineering case study demonstrating the early development stages of a wearable sensing technology.

The repository will document both successful and unsuccessful experiments where relevant. This approach is intentional: engineering development involves testing assumptions, identifying failures, troubleshooting problems, and iterating designs.

The value of the project therefore lies not only in the final prototype, but also in the documented development process.

---

## 9. Project Boundaries

The following areas are currently outside the scope of the initial project phase:

* Clinical validation.
* Medical diagnosis.
* Regulatory certification.
* Human clinical trials.
* Clinical-grade physiological measurement claims.
* Commercial product development.

Any physiological data collected during prototype testing will be used solely for engineering evaluation and signal acquisition experiments.

The prototype must not be used to support medical diagnosis, treatment decisions, or patient monitoring.

---

## 10. Future Development

Future versions of OpenTherm Health may explore:

* Improved miniaturisation.
* Custom PCB development.
* Wireless data transmission.
* Battery-powered operation.
* Improved enclosure design.
* Advanced signal processing.
* Sensor calibration studies.
* Extended thermal modelling.
* Usability evaluation.
* Expanded environmental monitoring.

These potential developments will only be considered after the successful completion and evaluation of the initial proof-of-concept.

---

## 11. Summary

OpenTherm Health is an independent biomedical engineering project investigating the integration of wearable sensing technologies with embedded systems and thermal performance evaluation.

The project recognises that wearable healthcare technologies involve complex interactions between sensors, electronics, software, physical design, and user requirements. Through iterative prototyping and experimental testing, OpenTherm Health aims to develop practical experience in the end-to-end engineering process while maintaining transparent documentation of design decisions, methods, results, limitations, and future improvements.

The next stage of the project is the definition of the intended use, system boundaries, and engineering design requirements.

